        <div id="which-to-buy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Which Asphalt Sealer Is Right for Your Driveway?
          </h2>

          <p>
            The condition of the surface picks the pail more reliably than the brand does. Walk the driveway, run the water test described in section 5, and then find the description below that matches what you see.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Sound and only slightly faded?</h3>

          <p>
            Choose Black Jack Drive-Maxx 1000 when appearance is the priority, or Latex-ite Airport Grade when you want the best mix of durability and price. Both leave a deep, even black on a smooth surface without much filling work beforehand.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Gray, rough and a little porous?</h3>

          <p>
            Reach for a filled product such as Jetcoat Premium or Latex-ite Airport Grade, and brush the first coat in. A surface like this drinks more than the label suggests, so buy extra, and close the cracks with Pli-Stix or a pourable filler before any sealer goes down.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Sealed recently and just needs a refresh?</h3>

          <p>
            Henry or Gardner is enough. A thin coat brings back the color and the water resistance without stacking up a thick layer that will crack later. Before you buy anything, check whether the driveway needs a coat this year at all; our guide on <a href="/how-often-should-you-seal-your-driveway/">how often to seal a driveway</a> explains how to judge it.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Is the driveway concrete rather than asphalt?</h3>

          <p>
            Then none of these belong on it. Asphalt sealer looks wrong on concrete, bonds badly and gets tracked into the house. Our guide to <a href="/best-driveway-sealer/">choosing a driveway sealer by surface type</a> covers both materials and the products made for each.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">A long rural driveway or a shared lane?</h3>

          <p>
            Get a contractor quote before buying a single pail. Beyond roughly 1,500 square feet, a crew working from bulk material often lands close to the price of a stack of premium pails, is done in a morning, and saves you two days of carrying, stirring and squeegeeing. If you would still rather do it yourself, Henry or Jetcoat keeps the material bill under control, and tackling half the driveway on each of two weekends keeps every session manageable for one or two people.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="worth-it" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Is It Worth Sealing an Old, Gray Asphalt Driveway?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Yes, if the structure underneath is sound and the damage is mostly on the surface. A rough rule: when less than about a tenth of the driveway shows alligator cracking or potholes, patching those spots and sealing the rest is usually money well spent. When large areas are breaking apart, the honest answer is moving toward resurfacing or replacement instead.
          </p>

          <p>
            The age of the driveway does not settle the question by itself. A 25-year-old driveway laid on a good base and sealed every so often can be in better condition than a 10-year-old one laid thin over poor fill. Judge what is in front of you, not the year it was paved. If you are still undecided, our breakdown of <a href="/sealing-asphalt-driveway-pros-and-cons/">the pros and cons of sealing an asphalt driveway</a> walks through when it pays off.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">A USEFUL RULE OF THUMB</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">On sound blacktop, sealer buys time. It cannot rebuild what has failed underneath.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">If a screwdriver sinks into the surface with little effort, if you can see base material through the cracks, or if a section gives underfoot, put the money into repair first. Seal once the patches have cured.</p>
          </div>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Should you seal a driveway you plan to replace soon?</h3>

          <p>
            There is a fair case for it. A coat slows raveling and keeps water out of the cracks, which can push the replacement date back, and it lifts curb appeal if the house is going on the market. Keep expectations realistic: you are buying a few more seasons, not a new driveway.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What if the driveway is new but already looks gray?</h3>

          <p>
            New blacktop has the opposite problem. Its oils need roughly six months to a year to cure before sealer will bond, and a new driveway that looks gray is often carrying surface dust and fines rather than oxidized binder. Our guide on <a href="/when-should-you-seal-a-new-asphalt-driveway/">when to seal a new asphalt driveway</a> explains how to tell the difference.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="what-sealer-does" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. What Does Asphalt Driveway Sealer Actually Do?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Sealer lays a fresh, sacrificial film of binder-like material over the pavement. That film blocks UV, sheds water, holds off gasoline and oil drips for a while, and brings back the black color. It is surface maintenance, and it works best on a driveway that is still structurally sound.
          </p>

          <p>
            To see why that matters, consider what asphalt is: stone glued together with a sticky petroleum binder. Over time, sun and oxygen harden that binder, which is why a freshly paved black driveway turns gray within a few years. Hardened binder becomes brittle, lets the fine material between the stones wash away, and opens small channels where water gets in. The sealer replaces the protective top layer that the weather has worn off.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Can sealer fix alligator cracking or potholes?</h3>

          <p>
            No. Sealer cannot restore structure. Alligator cracking (a web of connected cracks that resembles reptile skin), sunken patches, potholes and edges crumbling into the lawn all point to a problem in the base beneath the asphalt. A coat over the top improves the look for a season and changes nothing about the failure below. Those areas need patching, partial replacement or an overlay, with sealer applied afterward.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Which driveways benefit most?</h3>

          <p>
            A driveway a few years old, mostly intact and starting to fade gets the most out of a coat. One that is thirty years old and breaking up gets the least. If you cannot tell where yours sits, our look at <a href="/sealing-asphalt-driveway-pros-and-cons/">the real pros and cons of sealing asphalt</a> lays out the cases where sealing pays and the cases where it wastes money.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE QUICK TEST</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">On a dry day, tip a cup of water onto the driveway.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Water that beads up or runs off means the surface is still fairly tight, and sealing can wait. Water that darkens the asphalt and disappears within a minute or two means the binder has oxidized and the driveway is ready for a coat.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            New pavement is a separate case. Fresh asphalt needs its oils to cure out before sealer bonds well, and most paving contractors suggest waiting somewhere between six months and a year. Our guide on <a href="/when-should-you-seal-a-new-asphalt-driveway/">when to seal a new asphalt driveway</a> lists the signs that a new surface is ready.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="sealer-types" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. What Are the Main Types of Asphalt Driveway Sealer?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Almost every driveway sealer in a store fits one of four families: asphalt emulsion, polymer-modified emulsion, coal tar emulsion and oil-based (cutback). Knowing the family predicts how a pail will behave better than the brand or the years on the front, so identify it before comparing prices.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Type</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Base</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Best For</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Watch Out For</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Asphalt emulsion</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Asphalt, clay, water, emulsifier</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Most residential driveways</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Needs warm, dry weather to cure</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Polymer-modified emulsion</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Emulsion plus latex or rubber polymer</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Higher traffic, better scuff resistance</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Higher price per pail</td>
                  </tr>
