          <p>
            Treat those figures as rough ranges at the time of writing; prices move with region, product and how much crack repair is part of the job. The pattern holds regardless: the bigger the driveway, the better the contractor's numbers look.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What should you ask a sealcoating contractor?</h3>

          <p>
            Ask which product they use and whether it is coal tar or asphalt-based, how many coats they put down, whether crack filling is included, and how they deal with oil spots. Be cautious with door-to-door crews offering discounted material left over from a job nearby. That pitch is a well-known driveway scam, and the product is often watered down until it does almost nothing. Our explainer on <a href="/asphalt-sealcoating/">professional asphalt sealcoating</a> describes what a good job looks like.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            14. Common Mistakes With Asphalt Driveway Sealer
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sealing new asphalt too soon</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A new driveway is still giving off oils and stays softer than it will be a year later. Seal it early and the coat can trap those oils, bond weakly and scuff easily. Give it at least six months, ideally twelve, unless your paving contractor named a shorter wait for their specific mix.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Resealing every year out of habit</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Extra coats do not add protection. Each one builds thickness, and a heavy stack of old sealer eventually cracks and flakes, pulling the newer layers off with it. Most home driveways need a coat every two to three years. If water still beads and the surface still looks dark, wait another year.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Pouring without stirring</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Solids in a sealer pail pack down hard during shipping and storage. Skip the mixing and half the driveway gets watery sealer while the other half gets sludge, and the finish shows both. Run a drill paddle through each pail until the color and texture match from top to bottom.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Laying one thick coat to finish faster</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A heavy coat forms a skin while the layer below stays soft, so it cures slowly, picks up tire marks and cracks as it shrinks. Two thin coats with drying time between them give a tougher, more even surface. You spend an extra afternoon, not extra sealer, since two thin coats use about the same amount.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sealing without checking the hourly forecast</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Water-based sealer needs warm, dry weather to cure. A cold night or a thunderstorm in the first 24 hours can leave the coat soft, streaky or washed into the gutter. Look at the hourly forecast rather than the daily high, and wait for several dry days above the label minimum before opening a pail.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

