        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            15. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best asphalt driveway sealer for most homeowners?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For an ordinary driveway that has begun to fade, a filled, polymer-modified asphalt emulsion such as Latex-ite Airport Grade is the safest buy. It has the body to cover moderate wear, cleans up with water and is stocked widely enough to buy matching pails. On a smooth driveway where the finish matters most, Black Jack Drive-Maxx 1000 is the premium option.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does asphalt driveway sealer last?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Plan on two to three years on most home driveways, whatever number is printed on the pail. Heavy traffic, full sun, snowplows and a thin first coat shorten that; shade, light traffic and two careful coats stretch it. Use the water test to decide: once water soaks in instead of beading, the driveway is ready for another coat.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you put driveway sealer over cracks?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Over hairline cracks, yes, because a filled sealer bridges them. Anything wider than about an eighth of an inch should be cleaned out, filled with a dedicated crack filler and left to cure first. Sealer poured into an open crack just sinks, leaves a path for water, and the crack reappears within weeks.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long after sealing can you drive on a driveway?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most labels say 24 to 48 hours before cars, and longer when the weather is cool or humid. Foot traffic is usually fine after a few hours, once the surface is dry to the touch. For about the first week, avoid turning the steering wheel while the car is stationary, since twisting the tires on the soft coat leaves scuff marks.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How soon can you seal a new asphalt driveway?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Wait at least six months, and ideally a full year, unless your paving contractor gave a shorter interval for their mix. Fresh asphalt is still releasing oils, and sealer applied too early can trap them, bond poorly and scuff easily. A new driveway that already looks gray is often just dusty, not oxidized.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you use asphalt sealer on a concrete driveway?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. Asphalt sealer looks wrong on concrete, bonds poorly to it and tracks into the house, and it permanently stains any concrete it touches, which is why aprons and walkways should be taped off. A concrete driveway needs a sealer made for concrete; our guide to driveway sealers by surface covers those products.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What happens if it rains after you seal a driveway?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A shower in the first few hours can wash uncured sealer into the street and leave a thin, streaky surface that may need recoating. That is why most labels want a dry forecast for a full day or more after application. Check the hourly forecast, not only the daily high, before you open the first pail.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

